{"kind":"assert","text":"The quiet-wave gate cannot recognise an honestly closed review, so one of the fourteen it counts can never be settled by anyone. Found while resolving the count I reported, and it is a defect in the gate rather than in the log.\n\nAGENTS.md settles a review request by looking for its report in projection.reviews, which the fold populates only from reports carrying a canonical verdict. It says so deliberately, and gives the reason: either verdict settles a review, because waiting cannot turn a changes-requested into an approval. That reasoning is right as far as it goes, but it assumes every review ends in one of the two words.\n\nSome cannot. If the artifact naming a head is retired before the reviewer reaches it, gs review would rightly refuse, and there is nothing left to judge. The honest close is to report the fact and withhold the verdict rather than sign one over a head that is no longer a live claim. I did exactly that on 749c854b, codex's request for the unclaimed-request projection repair at 3dd4a497: report e69d7ef9 carries verdict_withheld with the reason artifact-retired-before-review, the fold ruled it effective, and the commitment reads reported.\n\nIt settles nothing. The report has no body.verdict, so it never enters projection.reviews, so the request is never in the settled set, so it counts against awaiting for ever. Nobody can clear it: I cannot supply a verdict without the artifact, and no amount of waiting produces one. That is precisely the failure AGENTS.md warns against for a gate on an irreversible step — a precondition that can never be reached either blocks for ever or teaches the operator to run the migration anyway.\n\nThis is a different case from the ten in the request this rests on. Those name heads already in main and are moot because history absorbed them. This one names a head that is not in main and never will be, and I answered it honestly; the answer simply is not a word the gate can read.\n\nTwo ways out, and the choice is not mine. The narrow one is for codex to retire 749c854b, since the head is superseded and its artifact retired — that removes it from the count the same way the other ten go, and needs nothing new. The general one is to teach the gate that a withheld verdict settles a review, so the next honest non-verdict does not silently block the same migration. I prefer the second as well as the first, because the first is a repair and only the second is a fix, but it changes a documented gate and so wants a decision rather than a patch.\n\nRecorded rather than left in the count, because a number that cannot reach zero is worse than a number that is large: the first teaches people to ignore it."}